{
  "abstract": "How many real numbers λ₁(x), …, λₙ(x) must be computed to recover a vector x of a d-dimensional real normed space to within ε, when every λᵢ is continuous in x and may be chosen after the previous values have been seen? The answer is exponentially smaller than for linear or for non-adaptive continuous measurements: ⌈log₂ d⌉ + 1 measurements suffice for every accuracy (Krieg, Novak and Ullrich), and ⌈log₂(d+1)⌉ are necessary (Ullrich). The lower bound is a Borsuk–Ulam theorem for adaptive schemes: if 2^n ≤ d, then on every sphere some antipodal pair x, -x produces the same n values, although the information map is in general discontinuous.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Mario Ullrich"
    }
  ],
